{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChFzTmYsss6g2NYX139PU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017CBASMVP4LYw6Q7NrLTGFR","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"PY=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-brenn-dev-brace/255658ca-f19b-460c-8892-2a477de97733/scratchpad/venv/bin/python; cd skill && $PY -I scripts/prescan.py ../agent | tail -5; $PY -I scripts/review_coverage.py inventory ../agent | tail -5; cd ..; sed -n 332,600p skill/references/build-time.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4180,"cache_read_input_tokens":18299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4180},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d82a645-8c23-4f20-8807-403197c900cd","uuid":"44fb8107-b2a3-4913-b2d2-7c0d6dc8eeb6","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:26:14.798Z","request_id":"req_011ChFzTmNTjfn6wmoW4RjoR","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017CBASMVP4LYw6Q7NrLTGFR":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-wrdef9oj && PY=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-brenn-dev-brace/255658ca-f19b-460c-8892-2a477de97733/scratchpad/venv/bin/python; cd skill && $PY -I scripts/prescan.py ../agent | tail -5; $PY -I scripts/review_coverage.py inventory ../agent | tail -5; cd ..; sed -n 332,600p skill/references/build-time.md"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_017CBASMVP4LYw6Q7NrLTGFR":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-wrdef9oj"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017CBASMVP4LYw6Q7NrLTGFR","type":"tool_result","content":"  B06: 1\n  B11: 6\n  E06: 1\n  R02: 1\n  R13: 6\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-wrdef9oj/skill/scripts/review_coverage.py\", line 17, in <module>\n    from prescan import walk, MAX_FILE_BYTES, COMMENT_LINE\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'prescan'\n## B06 · G1 · C4 — Approval integrity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Business owner, Security, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nTie each approval to the agent instance that asked, and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope. Record who approved it and when it expires. Show the reviewer what the action will do. Make approvals single-use, or set clear limits on a broader grant. When the action runs, check permissions and the relevant resource state again. Record each use of an approval in one step, so parallel calls can't go past the grant. Test changed arguments, a different agent instance, expired approvals, replay, parallel reuse, and approvers without authority. If the approval service fails, the action must not quietly go ahead.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nApprove a test action, then change its target or arguments. Separately, send it from a different agent instance, let the approval expire, use a reviewer without authority, and turn off the approval service. Each invalid try must fail. Race two requests that use the same approval, and change the destination's state after approval. Only approved uses of that exact action may run. Replays and changed state must be rejected.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Approval details, reviewer roles, gate decisions, resulting state.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B06.1** Each approval record is bound to the requesting agent instance and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope, and records the approver and an expiry time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the approval data model (schema, class, table, `approval`). 2) List its fields. 3) Answer no if it lacks any of agent instance ID, operation, target, exact arguments or hash, scope, approver, and expiry, or if approval is a boolean like `approved=True`.\n- **B06.2** When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where an approved action executes. 2) Check the code compares instance, operation, target, and arguments (or hash) against the stored approval and checks expiry at that moment. 3) Answer no if approval is checked only when requested, any field is not compared, arguments are re-read from the model after the check, or expiry is never checked.\n- **B06.3** Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where an approval is used or marked consumed. 2) Check it is one atomic step: transaction with lock, compare-and-set, unique constraint, or atomic decrement. 3) Answer no if it reads then writes separately, never marks use, or keeps the count in process memory that parallel workers do not share.\n- **B06.4** The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find approver checks (role list, `can_approve`, authority mapping). 2) Find rechecks of permissions and resource state at execution. 3) Answer no if any logged-in user can approve, the approver is not checked against the action, or permissions and resource state are not rechecked when the action runs.\n- **B06.5** If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls to the approval service. 2) Read their error, timeout, and exception handling. 3) Answer no if a `try`/`except` or `catch` logs and proceeds, the default on error or timeout is allow, or a missing response is treated as approved.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A high-impact action that never asks for approval at all → **B05**\n- A parent gaining a denied power by asking a stronger peer → **E08**\n- Harness or approval code changed in production without review → **B12**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- An approval record that stores agent instance ID, operation, target, exact arguments (or their hash), scope, approver, and expiry.\n- At execution, the harness compares the call against the stored approval and rechecks permissions and resource state.\n- Single-use approvals consumed atomically, for example a DB transaction, compare-and-set, or unique constraint on approval use.\n- Approver authority checked against a role list, and the reviewer shown a rendered preview of the action.\n- Fail-closed handling when the approval service errors or times out.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Approval stored as a boolean flag (for example `approved=True`) with no link to arguments or instance.\n- Approval checked once, then the model can change arguments before the call runs.\n- No expiry on approvals, or approvals reused across sessions or agent instances.\n- `try: check_approval() except: proceed` or a default of allow when the approval service is down.\n- Approval use counted with read-then-write logic that parallel calls can race.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Approval service or human-in-the-loop module\n- Tool execution wrapper and hooks\n- Approval data model and database schema\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Behavior under changed args, other instances, expiry, replay, races, unauthorized approvers, and service outage. Recipe B06 runs each case.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no actions that need approval under B05, with that inventory recorded.\n\n## B07 · G2 · C4 — Hard execution budgets\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Business owner, Infrastructure, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nEnforce limits outside the model on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and how many sub-agents it can start. Also set spend and rate quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the whole fleet. That way, many small runs can't use up shared capacity. Hit each limit in a test, and record how the run stops or escalates.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSet small test budgets and go over each one separately, including budgets for nested agents. The run must stop or escalate at the set limit. Check that actual usage, and any overshoot from calls already in progress, stays within the tolerance you set in advance. Start many small runs at once under one user or service and one tenant. The overall quotas must stop them.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Budget settings, usage counters, stop events.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B07.1** Limits on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and number of sub-agents started are set in code or config.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for `max_turns`, `recursion_limit`, `max_iterations`, `timeout`, `max_tokens`, cost or budget caps, rate limits, sub-agent caps. 2) Check each of the six limits has a value. 3) Answer no if any is missing, set to unlimited (`None`, `0`, `-1`, `inf`), or the loop is `while True` with no cap.\n- **B07.2** The harness or a gateway outside the model enforces each limit and stops or escalates the run when it is hit; the model cannot raise, reset, or skip them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Inventory every main/helper model invocation, tool call and retry. 2) For each, locate token/cost/rate enforcement and the stop/escalation branch. 3) Trace a boundary case: enforcement/reservation before work, or a documented finite overshoot bound with enforcement. 4) Answer no for unmetered helpers, uncounted retries, unbounded overshoot, model-controlled limits or catches that continue. Small max_tokens and timeouts do not substitute for shared token/cost accounting.\n- **B07.3** Budgets apply to nested sub-agents, so their usage counts against the limits.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawning (`spawn`, `Task`, `handoff`, `create_agent`). 2) Check child runs share or deduct from the parent budget and depth is capped. 3) Answer no if each child gets a fresh budget, nested spawns are uncounted, or depth and count are unlimited.\n- **B07.4** Spend and rate quotas also apply per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, counted across runs, not only within one run.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace every model/tool path into persistent per-user/service, per-tenant and fleet spend/rate quotas. 2) Trace concurrent small runs and charging order; distinguish shared counters from enforcement. 3) Answer no if required calls escape accounting or quotas can be exceeded without a finite bound and stop. Answer unknown when the relevant gateway/provider is outside the repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Budget or cross-agent abuse alerts not reaching the team that can act → **E12**\n- Unusual fan-out or resource use going undetected → **R09**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Limits enforced by the harness, not the model: `max_turns`, `recursion_limit` (LangGraph), `max_iterations` (LangChain), wall-clock timeouts, `max_tokens`, cost caps.\n- Tool-call rate limits and a cap on how many sub-agents a run can start, including nested ones.\n- Quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, for example in an API gateway, LLM proxy (LiteLLM budgets), or provider spend limits.\n- A defined stop or escalation action when a limit is hit.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Loop is `while True` or `while not done` with no iteration or time cap.\n- Budgets stated only in the system prompt ('stop after 10 steps').\n- Limits per run only, with no per-tenant or fleet quota, so many small runs can drain shared capacity.\n- Sub-agent spawning with no depth or count limit.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Agent loop and runner config\n- LLM gateway/proxy config and provider budget settings\n- Sub-agent or task spawning code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That each limit trips at its set value with overshoot inside tolerance, and that fleet quotas stop many concurrent runs. Recipe B07.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B08 · G2 · C1 — Environment isolation\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nWrite down every system and data store the agent can reach. Test that it has no path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Enforce this in the infrastructure, not only in the prompt. For each allowed connection, also check input and response validation under R01–R02, including API responses and messages from connected services. Being allowed on the network does not make their content trusted.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nFrom inside the agent's runtime, try test endpoints that stand for forbidden networks, host and admin services, and tenant B. Check the actual routes and isolation policies, not just failed requests. An endpoint that happens to be down doesn't prove isolation.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Policy and route exports, connection results, isolation-denial logs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B08.1** Network policy or security groups default-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find NetworkPolicy, security groups, firewall rules, docker-compose networks. 2) Check for a default deny and named allowed peers. 3) Answer no if the agent's namespace has no policy, rules allow all, `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad CIDR is allowed, or allowed peers go beyond the listed systems. Answer unknown if network config lives outside this repo.\n- **B08.2** No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\n- **B08.3** Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where isolation is defined. 2) Check it is in infrastructure (NetworkPolicy, VPC, IAM, separate namespace or account). 3) Answer no if it relies on prompt text or agent code (URL checks, host lists in the harness) that the model's output can steer, with no infrastructure rule behind it.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations → **B09**\n- Container running as root or shipping a shell it doesn't need → **B11**\n- Content from an allowed service trusted without validation → **R01**\n- Agent reaching an approved MCP server directly instead of through the gateway → **E06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Kubernetes NetworkPolicy with default deny and named allowed peers, or cloud security groups/VPC rules that admit only approved systems.\n- Agent runs in its own namespace, account, or project, apart from production data stores and admin systems.\n- Tenant separation in infrastructure, for example per-tenant namespaces, databases, or credentials.\n- A written list of every system and data store the agent can reach.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No NetworkPolicy in the agent's namespace, or a policy that allows all ingress/egress.\n- `hostNetwork: true`, `hostPID: true`, or the Docker socket (`/var/run/docker.sock`) mounted.\n- Agent shares a network or VPC with production admin consoles or databases with no rule between them.\n- Isolation stated only in the prompt ('do not access production').\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Kubernetes manifests and Helm charts (NetworkPolicy, namespaces)\n- Terraform networking (VPC, subnets, security groups, firewall rules)\n- docker-compose networks and volume mounts\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Actual routes and policies in the running environment. Recipe B08: probe forbidden networks, host services, and tenant B from inside the runtime and export the live policies.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B09 · G2 · C1 — Egress allowlist\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nAllow only the outside destinations the task needs. Test a blocked destination and ways around the block, such as going straight past a proxy or gateway. Cover redirects, DNS changes and rebinding, IPv4 and IPv6, and cloud metadata or loopback addresses where they apply. An allowed domain can still host an attacker's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nCall one allowed and one forbidden test destination. Include direct routes around the proxy or gateway, redirect chains, DNS changes, IPv6, and test metadata and loopback addresses. Also try a forbidden resource on an allowed service. The allowed request must work. Forbidden requests must fail, and the block must reach the named operator within the alert time limit.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Egress rules, gateway and network logs, alert receipt time.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B09.1** Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find egress rules (NetworkPolicy egress, Cilium FQDN, proxy ACLs, cloud firewall). 2) Check outbound is default deny with a destination allowlist. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or broad CIDRs, no egress rules exist, or the allowlist is only in app code the agent runs. Answer unknown if egress is set outside this repo.\n- **B09.2** Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the proxy is enforced by network policy or only by `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` env. 2) Check IPv6 egress rules. 3) Answer no if pods can reach the internet except via the proxy, the proxy is only an env var, `NO_PROXY` is broad, or IPv6 is not restricted.\n- **B09.3** Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find fetch and HTTP client code (`requests`, `fetch`, `http.Client`, `follow_redirects`, `allow_redirects`). 2) Check each redirect hop and resolved IP is checked. 3) Answer no if redirects are followed unchecked, the check uses the hostname but not the resolved IP, or 169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, or 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1 are not blocked.\n- **B09.4** Allowed destinations are limited by resource, tenant, or path where needed, not only by domain.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List allowed destinations that are shared services (S3, GitHub, storage, paste sites). 2) Check rules limit bucket, account, repo, tenant, or path. 3) Answer no if a shared service is allowed by domain or wildcard (`*.s3.amazonaws.com`) alone, or path checks use string prefix without normalising.\n- **B09.5** Each blocked request is logged and routed as an alert to a named operator.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where blocked requests are logged (proxy deny log, policy audit, app code). 2) Find the alert route to a named operator. 3) Answer no if blocks are not logged, only allowed requests are logged, or no alert route names an operator. Answer unknown if alerting lives outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Data leaking through client-side Markdown images or link previews → **R03**\n- Network path to internal production or admin systems → **B08**\n- A loosened egress rule in the running setup raising no alert → **C04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Egress allowlist by destination, for example an egress proxy or gateway config, Cilium/Calico FQDN policies, or cloud firewall rules naming hosts.\n- Direct routes around the proxy blocked (NetworkPolicy egress only to the proxy), not just `HTTP_PROXY` env vars.\n- Metadata and loopback addresses blocked (169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, 127.0.0.0/8), with IPv6 covered.\n- Fetch tools that resolve and pin DNS, re-check redirects, and limit which paths, accounts, or tenants on an allowed domain they may hit.\n- Blocked-egress events logged and routed to a named operator.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Egress allowed to `0.0.0.0/0` or no egress rules at all.\n- Proxy set only through `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY`, which code can ignore.\n- HTTP client with `follow_redirects=True` and no re-check of each redirect target.\n- Allowlist by domain only on a shared service (for example any `*.s3.amazonaws.com` bucket or any GitHub repo) with no limit on account or resource.\n- A fetch or browse tool that takes any model-supplied URL.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- NetworkPolicy/CiliumNetworkPolicy egress rules\n- Egress proxy or gateway config (Squid, Envoy, cloud NAT/firewall)\n- HTTP fetch, browser, and webhook tool code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That bypass attempts fail and the block alert reaches the operator in time. Recipe B09 tests redirects, DNS rebinding, IPv6, metadata, and forbidden resources on allowed services.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B10 · G2 · C3 — Pinned, verified image\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nPin the container by digest. Before it runs, check its digest and signature against an approved signing key, or an approved certificate identity and issuer. Keep its build provenance (the record of how it was built) and its list of dependencies. Check provenance fields such as the builder, source repository, build type, and the parameters set from outside the build (SLSA `externalParameters`) against what you expect. Keep the verifier patched. Cosign versions before 2.6.2 and 3.0.4 could accept a bundle whose transparency-log entry didn't match the artifact. Confirm that the admission check covers production namespaces and is set to enforce, not just audit. Show that an unapproved or changed image is rejected.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nDeploy the approved signed digest. Then try an unsigned image, an image signed by an unapproved identity, and a changed digest. Only the approved artifact may be admitted. Also deploy a correctly signed image built from an unexpected source repository or with changed build settings. Provenance checks must reject it. Confirm the verifier version. Confirm the policy is set to enforce, not just audit, and covers production namespaces. Confirm an image that matches no policy is denied. Check that the running digest matches the signed build record.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Signature and admission results, provenance check output, verifier version, policy mode and scope, build provenance, running image digest.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B10.1** Production manifests reference images by digest (`@sha256:`), not by tag alone.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every `image:` field in manifests, Helm values, compose, and Terraform. 2) Check each uses `@sha256:`. 3) Answer no if any production image uses only a tag (`:latest`, `:v1`), or a template defaults to a tag when the digest value is empty.\n- **B10.2** An admission policy verifies the signature against a pinned key, or a pinned certificate identity and issuer, with no wildcard identity or issuer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find admission policies (Kyverno `verifyImages`, `ClusterImagePolicy`, Gatekeeper). 2) Check the key or certificate identity and issuer are pinned. 3) Answer no if identity or issuer uses `.*` or wildcard regex, issuer is unset, or no admission policy exists. Answer unknown if admission config is outside this repo.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d82a645-8c23-4f20-8807-403197c900cd","uuid":"5dd04b19-bc4a-428a-acc3-cab5a6cc0cce","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:26:15.672Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  B06: 1\n  B11: 6\n  E06: 1\n  R02: 1\n  R13: 6\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-wrdef9oj/skill/scripts/review_coverage.py\", line 17, in <module>\n    from prescan import walk, MAX_FILE_BYTES, COMMENT_LINE\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'prescan'\n## B06 · G1 · C4 — Approval integrity\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Business owner, Security, Infrastructure.\n\n### Requirement\n\nTie each approval to the agent instance that asked, and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope. Record who approved it and when it expires. Show the reviewer what the action will do. Make approvals single-use, or set clear limits on a broader grant. When the action runs, check permissions and the relevant resource state again. Record each use of an approval in one step, so parallel calls can't go past the grant. Test changed arguments, a different agent instance, expired approvals, replay, parallel reuse, and approvers without authority. If the approval service fails, the action must not quietly go ahead.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nApprove a test action, then change its target or arguments. Separately, send it from a different agent instance, let the approval expire, use a reviewer without authority, and turn off the approval service. Each invalid try must fail. Race two requests that use the same approval, and change the destination's state after approval. Only approved uses of that exact action may run. Replays and changed state must be rejected.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Approval details, reviewer roles, gate decisions, resulting state.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B06.1** Each approval record is bound to the requesting agent instance and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope, and records the approver and an expiry time.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find the approval data model (schema, class, table, `approval`). 2) List its fields. 3) Answer no if it lacks any of agent instance ID, operation, target, exact arguments or hash, scope, approver, and expiry, or if approval is a boolean like `approved=True`.\n- **B06.2** When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where an approved action executes. 2) Check the code compares instance, operation, target, and arguments (or hash) against the stored approval and checks expiry at that moment. 3) Answer no if approval is checked only when requested, any field is not compared, arguments are re-read from the model after the check, or expiry is never checked.\n- **B06.3** Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where an approval is used or marked consumed. 2) Check it is one atomic step: transaction with lock, compare-and-set, unique constraint, or atomic decrement. 3) Answer no if it reads then writes separately, never marks use, or keeps the count in process memory that parallel workers do not share.\n- **B06.4** The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find approver checks (role list, `can_approve`, authority mapping). 2) Find rechecks of permissions and resource state at execution. 3) Answer no if any logged-in user can approve, the approver is not checked against the action, or permissions and resource state are not rechecked when the action runs.\n- **B06.5** If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find calls to the approval service. 2) Read their error, timeout, and exception handling. 3) Answer no if a `try`/`except` or `catch` logs and proceeds, the default on error or timeout is allow, or a missing response is treated as approved.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- A high-impact action that never asks for approval at all → **B05**\n- A parent gaining a denied power by asking a stronger peer → **E08**\n- Harness or approval code changed in production without review → **B12**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- An approval record that stores agent instance ID, operation, target, exact arguments (or their hash), scope, approver, and expiry.\n- At execution, the harness compares the call against the stored approval and rechecks permissions and resource state.\n- Single-use approvals consumed atomically, for example a DB transaction, compare-and-set, or unique constraint on approval use.\n- Approver authority checked against a role list, and the reviewer shown a rendered preview of the action.\n- Fail-closed handling when the approval service errors or times out.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Approval stored as a boolean flag (for example `approved=True`) with no link to arguments or instance.\n- Approval checked once, then the model can change arguments before the call runs.\n- No expiry on approvals, or approvals reused across sessions or agent instances.\n- `try: check_approval() except: proceed` or a default of allow when the approval service is down.\n- Approval use counted with read-then-write logic that parallel calls can race.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Approval service or human-in-the-loop module\n- Tool execution wrapper and hooks\n- Approval data model and database schema\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Behavior under changed args, other instances, expiry, replay, races, unauthorized approvers, and service outage. Recipe B06 runs each case.\n\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no actions that need approval under B05, with that inventory recorded.\n\n## B07 · G2 · C4 — Hard execution budgets\n\n**Lead:** Agent team. **Shares:** Business owner, Infrastructure, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nEnforce limits outside the model on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and how many sub-agents it can start. Also set spend and rate quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the whole fleet. That way, many small runs can't use up shared capacity. Hit each limit in a test, and record how the run stops or escalates.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nSet small test budgets and go over each one separately, including budgets for nested agents. The run must stop or escalate at the set limit. Check that actual usage, and any overshoot from calls already in progress, stays within the tolerance you set in advance. Start many small runs at once under one user or service and one tenant. The overall quotas must stop them.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Budget settings, usage counters, stop events.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B07.1** Limits on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and number of sub-agents started are set in code or config.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search for `max_turns`, `recursion_limit`, `max_iterations`, `timeout`, `max_tokens`, cost or budget caps, rate limits, sub-agent caps. 2) Check each of the six limits has a value. 3) Answer no if any is missing, set to unlimited (`None`, `0`, `-1`, `inf`), or the loop is `while True` with no cap.\n- **B07.2** The harness or a gateway outside the model enforces each limit and stops or escalates the run when it is hit; the model cannot raise, reset, or skip them.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Inventory every main/helper model invocation, tool call and retry. 2) For each, locate token/cost/rate enforcement and the stop/escalation branch. 3) Trace a boundary case: enforcement/reservation before work, or a documented finite overshoot bound with enforcement. 4) Answer no for unmetered helpers, uncounted retries, unbounded overshoot, model-controlled limits or catches that continue. Small max_tokens and timeouts do not substitute for shared token/cost accounting.\n- **B07.3** Budgets apply to nested sub-agents, so their usage counts against the limits.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find sub-agent spawning (`spawn`, `Task`, `handoff`, `create_agent`). 2) Check child runs share or deduct from the parent budget and depth is capped. 3) Answer no if each child gets a fresh budget, nested spawns are uncounted, or depth and count are unlimited.\n- **B07.4** Spend and rate quotas also apply per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, counted across runs, not only within one run.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Trace every model/tool path into persistent per-user/service, per-tenant and fleet spend/rate quotas. 2) Trace concurrent small runs and charging order; distinguish shared counters from enforcement. 3) Answer no if required calls escape accounting or quotas can be exceeded without a finite bound and stop. Answer unknown when the relevant gateway/provider is outside the repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Budget or cross-agent abuse alerts not reaching the team that can act → **E12**\n- Unusual fan-out or resource use going undetected → **R09**\n- Context size not logged for each action decision → **R07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Limits enforced by the harness, not the model: `max_turns`, `recursion_limit` (LangGraph), `max_iterations` (LangChain), wall-clock timeouts, `max_tokens`, cost caps.\n- Tool-call rate limits and a cap on how many sub-agents a run can start, including nested ones.\n- Quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, for example in an API gateway, LLM proxy (LiteLLM budgets), or provider spend limits.\n- A defined stop or escalation action when a limit is hit.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Loop is `while True` or `while not done` with no iteration or time cap.\n- Budgets stated only in the system prompt ('stop after 10 steps').\n- Limits per run only, with no per-tenant or fleet quota, so many small runs can drain shared capacity.\n- Sub-agent spawning with no depth or count limit.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Agent loop and runner config\n- LLM gateway/proxy config and provider budget settings\n- Sub-agent or task spawning code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That each limit trips at its set value with overshoot inside tolerance, and that fleet quotas stop many concurrent runs. Recipe B07.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B08 · G2 · C1 — Environment isolation\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security.\n\n### Requirement\n\nWrite down every system and data store the agent can reach. Test that it has no path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Enforce this in the infrastructure, not only in the prompt. For each allowed connection, also check input and response validation under R01–R02, including API responses and messages from connected services. Being allowed on the network does not make their content trusted.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nFrom inside the agent's runtime, try test endpoints that stand for forbidden networks, host and admin services, and tenant B. Check the actual routes and isolation policies, not just failed requests. An endpoint that happens to be down doesn't prove isolation.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Policy and route exports, connection results, isolation-denial logs.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B08.1** Network policy or security groups default-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find NetworkPolicy, security groups, firewall rules, docker-compose networks. 2) Check for a default deny and named allowed peers. 3) Answer no if the agent's namespace has no policy, rules allow all, `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad CIDR is allowed, or allowed peers go beyond the listed systems. Answer unknown if network config lives outside this repo.\n- **B08.2** No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\n- **B08.3** Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where isolation is defined. 2) Check it is in infrastructure (NetworkPolicy, VPC, IAM, separate namespace or account). 3) Answer no if it relies on prompt text or agent code (URL checks, host lists in the harness) that the model's output can steer, with no infrastructure rule behind it.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations → **B09**\n- Container running as root or shipping a shell it doesn't need → **B11**\n- Content from an allowed service trusted without validation → **R01**\n- Agent reaching an approved MCP server directly instead of through the gateway → **E06**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Kubernetes NetworkPolicy with default deny and named allowed peers, or cloud security groups/VPC rules that admit only approved systems.\n- Agent runs in its own namespace, account, or project, apart from production data stores and admin systems.\n- Tenant separation in infrastructure, for example per-tenant namespaces, databases, or credentials.\n- A written list of every system and data store the agent can reach.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No NetworkPolicy in the agent's namespace, or a policy that allows all ingress/egress.\n- `hostNetwork: true`, `hostPID: true`, or the Docker socket (`/var/run/docker.sock`) mounted.\n- Agent shares a network or VPC with production admin consoles or databases with no rule between them.\n- Isolation stated only in the prompt ('do not access production').\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Kubernetes manifests and Helm charts (NetworkPolicy, namespaces)\n- Terraform networking (VPC, subnets, security groups, firewall rules)\n- docker-compose networks and volume mounts\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Actual routes and policies in the running environment. Recipe B08: probe forbidden networks, host services, and tenant B from inside the runtime and export the live policies.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B09 · G2 · C1 — Egress allowlist\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Operations.\n\n### Requirement\n\nAllow only the outside destinations the task needs. Test a blocked destination and ways around the block, such as going straight past a proxy or gateway. Cover redirects, DNS changes and rebinding, IPv4 and IPv6, and cloud metadata or loopback addresses where they apply. An allowed domain can still host an attacker's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nCall one allowed and one forbidden test destination. Include direct routes around the proxy or gateway, redirect chains, DNS changes, IPv6, and test metadata and loopback addresses. Also try a forbidden resource on an allowed service. The allowed request must work. Forbidden requests must fail, and the block must reach the named operator within the alert time limit.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Egress rules, gateway and network logs, alert receipt time.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B09.1** Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find egress rules (NetworkPolicy egress, Cilium FQDN, proxy ACLs, cloud firewall). 2) Check outbound is default deny with a destination allowlist. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or broad CIDRs, no egress rules exist, or the allowlist is only in app code the agent runs. Answer unknown if egress is set outside this repo.\n- **B09.2** Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the proxy is enforced by network policy or only by `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` env. 2) Check IPv6 egress rules. 3) Answer no if pods can reach the internet except via the proxy, the proxy is only an env var, `NO_PROXY` is broad, or IPv6 is not restricted.\n- **B09.3** Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find fetch and HTTP client code (`requests`, `fetch`, `http.Client`, `follow_redirects`, `allow_redirects`). 2) Check each redirect hop and resolved IP is checked. 3) Answer no if redirects are followed unchecked, the check uses the hostname but not the resolved IP, or 169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, or 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1 are not blocked.\n- **B09.4** Allowed destinations are limited by resource, tenant, or path where needed, not only by domain.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) List allowed destinations that are shared services (S3, GitHub, storage, paste sites). 2) Check rules limit bucket, account, repo, tenant, or path. 3) Answer no if a shared service is allowed by domain or wildcard (`*.s3.amazonaws.com`) alone, or path checks use string prefix without normalising.\n- **B09.5** Each blocked request is logged and routed as an alert to a named operator.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where blocked requests are logged (proxy deny log, policy audit, app code). 2) Find the alert route to a named operator. 3) Answer no if blocks are not logged, only allowed requests are logged, or no alert route names an operator. Answer unknown if alerting lives outside this repo.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Data leaking through client-side Markdown images or link previews → **R03**\n- Network path to internal production or admin systems → **B08**\n- A loosened egress rule in the running setup raising no alert → **C04**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Egress allowlist by destination, for example an egress proxy or gateway config, Cilium/Calico FQDN policies, or cloud firewall rules naming hosts.\n- Direct routes around the proxy blocked (NetworkPolicy egress only to the proxy), not just `HTTP_PROXY` env vars.\n- Metadata and loopback addresses blocked (169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, 127.0.0.0/8), with IPv6 covered.\n- Fetch tools that resolve and pin DNS, re-check redirects, and limit which paths, accounts, or tenants on an allowed domain they may hit.\n- Blocked-egress events logged and routed to a named operator.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Egress allowed to `0.0.0.0/0` or no egress rules at all.\n- Proxy set only through `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY`, which code can ignore.\n- HTTP client with `follow_redirects=True` and no re-check of each redirect target.\n- Allowlist by domain only on a shared service (for example any `*.s3.amazonaws.com` bucket or any GitHub repo) with no limit on account or resource.\n- A fetch or browse tool that takes any model-supplied URL.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- NetworkPolicy/CiliumNetworkPolicy egress rules\n- Egress proxy or gateway config (Squid, Envoy, cloud NAT/firewall)\n- HTTP fetch, browser, and webhook tool code\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That bypass attempts fail and the block alert reaches the operator in time. Recipe B09 tests redirects, DNS rebinding, IPv6, metadata, and forbidden resources on allowed services.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B10 · G2 · C3 — Pinned, verified image\n\n**Lead:** Infrastructure. **Shares:** Security, Agent team.\n\n### Requirement\n\nPin the container by digest. Before it runs, check its digest and signature against an approved signing key, or an approved certificate identity and issuer. Keep its build provenance (the record of how it was built) and its list of dependencies. Check provenance fields such as the builder, source repository, build type, and the parameters set from outside the build (SLSA `externalParameters`) against what you expect. Keep the verifier patched. Cosign versions before 2.6.2 and 3.0.4 could accept a bundle whose transparency-log entry didn't match the artifact. Confirm that the admission check covers production namespaces and is set to enforce, not just audit. Show that an unapproved or changed image is rejected.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nDeploy the approved signed digest. Then try an unsigned image, an image signed by an unapproved identity, and a changed digest. Only the approved artifact may be admitted. Also deploy a correctly signed image built from an unexpected source repository or with changed build settings. Provenance checks must reject it. Confirm the verifier version. Confirm the policy is set to enforce, not just audit, and covers production namespaces. Confirm an image that matches no policy is denied. Check that the running digest matches the signed build record.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Signature and admission results, provenance check output, verifier version, policy mode and scope, build provenance, running image digest.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nRate likely_pass only if every check below is true, with a file and line for each:\n\n- **B10.1** Production manifests reference images by digest (`@sha256:`), not by tag alone.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find every `image:` field in manifests, Helm values, compose, and Terraform. 2) Check each uses `@sha256:`. 3) Answer no if any production image uses only a tag (`:latest`, `:v1`), or a template defaults to a tag when the digest value is empty.\n- **B10.2** An admission policy verifies the signature against a pinned key, or a pinned certificate identity and issuer, with no wildcard identity or issuer.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find admission policies (Kyverno `verifyImages`, `ClusterImagePolicy`, Gatekeeper). 2) Check the key or certificate identity and issuer are pinned. 3) Answer no if identity or issuer uses `.*` or wildcard regex, issuer is unset, or no admission policy exists. Answer unknown if admission config is outside this repo.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017CBASMVP4LYw6Q7NrLTGFR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChFzTmYsss6g2NYX139PU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017uyaaUXK1twvW8uyTu3SDZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-wrdef9oj/agent && cat -n src/approvals.py src/agent.py src/budgets.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4180,"cache_read_input_tokens":18299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4180},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d82a645-8c23-4f20-8807-403197c900cd","uuid":"c4781750-d445-4281-9b01-daa34935d5a7","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:26:15.826Z","request_id":"req_011ChFzTmNTjfn6wmoW4RjoR","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017uyaaUXK1twvW8uyTu3SDZ":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-wrdef9oj/agent && cat -n src/approvals.py src/agent.py src/budgets.py"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017uyaaUXK1twvW8uyTu3SDZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t\"\"\"Approval records for consequential tool calls.\n     2\t\n     3\tAn approval is bound to one agent instance, one operation, one target and the SHA-256 of\n     4\tthe canonical arguments. Only the employee on whose behalf the run acts may approve, they\n     5\tsee a rendered description of the exact action, and each approval is consumed once.\n     6\t\"\"\"\n     7\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     8\t\n     9\timport hashlib\n    10\timport json\n    11\timport uuid\n    12\tfrom datetime import timedelta\n    13\tfrom typing import Any\n    14\t\n    15\timport asyncpg\n    16\t\n    17\tfrom .audit import RunContext\n    18\t\n    19\t\n    20\tclass ApprovalInvalid(Exception):\n    21\t    pass\n    22\t\n    23\t\n    24\tdef args_digest(args: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    25\t    canonical = json.dumps(args, sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"), ensure_ascii=False)\n    26\t    return hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode()).hexdigest()\n    27\t\n    28\t\n    29\tdef describe(operation: str, target: str, args: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    30\t    if operation == \"hris/file_leave_request\":\n    31\t        return (f\"Submit a {args['leave_type']} leave request from {args['start_date']} to {args['end_date']} \"\n    32\t                f\"for {target}. Your manager will be asked to approve it in HRIS.\")\n    33\t    if operation == \"helpdesk/create_ticket\":\n    34\t        return f\"Send this summary to an HR advisor ({args['category']}):\\n\\n{args['summary']}\"\n    35\t    raise ValueError(operation)\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tclass ApprovalStore:\n    39\t    def __init__(self, pool: asyncpg.Pool, ttl_seconds: int):\n    40\t        self._pool = pool\n    41\t        self._ttl = timedelta(seconds=ttl_seconds)\n    42\t\n    43\t    async def request(self, ctx: RunContext, operation: str, target: str, args: dict[str, Any], tool_use_id: str) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    44\t        approval_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    45\t        text = describe(operation, target, args)\n    46\t        await self._pool.execute(\n    47\t            \"\"\"INSERT INTO approvals.grants\n    48\t                 (approval_id, run_id, agent_instance_id, subject, operation, target, args_sha256, args,\n    49\t                  tool_use_id, description, requested_at, expires_at)\n    50\t               VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5, $6, $7, $8::jsonb, $9, $10, now(), now() + $11)\"\"\",\n    51\t            approval_id, ctx.run_id, ctx.agent_instance_id, ctx.subject, operation, target,\n    52\t            args_digest(args), json.dumps(args), tool_use_id, text, self._ttl,\n    53\t        )\n    54\t        return approval_id, text\n    55\t\n    56\t    async def confirm(self, approval_id: str, approver: str) -> None:\n    57\t        \"\"\"Called from the employee's authenticated session. Approver must be the run's subject.\"\"\"\n    58\t        row = await self._pool.fetchrow(\n    59\t            \"\"\"UPDATE approvals.grants SET approved_by = $2, approved_at = now()\n    60\t                WHERE approval_id = $1 AND subject = $2 AND approved_at IS NULL AND expires_at > now()\n    61\t                RETURNING approval_id\"\"\",\n    62\t            approval_id, approver,\n    63\t        )\n    64\t        if row is None:\n    65\t            raise ApprovalInvalid(\"not found, expired, already decided, or approver lacks authority\")\n    66\t\n    67\t    async def consume(self, ctx: RunContext, approval_id: str, operation: str, target: str, args: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    68\t        \"\"\"Atomically mark the approval used. Every binding is re-checked in the same statement.\"\"\"\n    69\t        row = await self._pool.fetchrow(\n    70\t            \"\"\"UPDATE approvals.grants SET used_at = now()\n    71\t                WHERE approval_id = $1\n    72\t                  AND used_at IS NULL\n    73\t                  AND approved_at IS NOT NULL\n    74\t                  AND approved_by = subject\n    75\t                  AND expires_at > now()\n    76\t                  AND agent_instance_id = $2\n    77\t                  AND subject = $3\n    78\t                  AND operation = $4\n    79\t                  AND target = $5\n    80\t                  AND args_sha256 = $6\n    81\t                RETURNING approval_id, approved_by, approved_at, expires_at\"\"\",\n    82\t            approval_id, ctx.agent_instance_id, ctx.subject, operation, target, args_digest(args),\n    83\t        )\n    84\t        if row is None:\n    85\t            raise ApprovalInvalid(\"approval does not match this action, was used, or expired\")\n    86\t        return dict(row)\n    87\t\n    88\t    async def pending(self, approval_id: str) -> dict[str, Any] | None:\n    89\t        row = await self._pool.fetchrow(\n    90\t            \"SELECT run_id, operation, target, args, tool_use_id FROM approvals.grants WHERE approval_id = $1\",\n    91\t            approval_id,\n    92\t        )\n    93\t        if row is None:\n    94\t            return None\n    95\t        out = dict(row)\n    96\t        out[\"args\"] = json.loads(out[\"args\"])\n    97\t        return out\n     1\t\"\"\"Agent loop for the HR helpdesk.\"\"\"\n     2\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     3\t\n     4\timport json\n     5\timport logging\n     6\timport pathlib\n     7\timport uuid\n     8\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n     9\tfrom datetime import date\n    10\tfrom typing import Any\n    11\t\n    12\tfrom anthropic import AsyncAnthropic\n    13\tfrom opentelemetry import trace\n    14\t\n    15\tfrom .approvals import ApprovalInvalid, ApprovalStore\n    16\tfrom .audit import AuditWriter, RunContext\n    17\tfrom .budgets import BudgetExceeded, QuotaStore, RunBudget\n    18\tfrom .gateway_client import GatewayClient, ToolDenied, ToolError\n    19\tfrom .killswitch import StopControl\n    20\tfrom .redact import redact\n    21\tfrom .retrieval import PolicyIndex\n    22\tfrom .tools import ToolRegistry, ToolSpec\n    23\tfrom .validation import ValidationRejected, validate_chunk, validate_tool_args, validate_tool_output\n    24\t\n    25\tlog = logging.getLogger(\"hr_agent.loop\")\n    26\ttracer = trace.get_tracer(\"hr_agent\")\n    27\t\n    28\tSYSTEM_PROMPT = (pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"prompts\" / \"system.md\").read_text()\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t@dataclass\n    32\tclass RunState:\n    33\t    messages: list[dict[str, Any]]\n    34\t    budget: RunBudget\n    35\t    subject_token: str\n    36\t    status: str = \"running\"\n    37\t    reply: str | None = None\n    38\t    pending_approval: dict[str, Any] | None = None\n    39\t    last_step: str | None = None\n    40\t    subagents_started: int = 0\n    41\t    extra: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tclass Agent:\n    45\t    def __init__(self, cfg: dict[str, Any], registry: ToolRegistry, llm: AsyncAnthropic, gateway: GatewayClient,\n    46\t                 index: PolicyIndex, approvals: ApprovalStore, quotas: QuotaStore, audit: AuditWriter, stop: StopControl):\n    47\t        self.cfg, self.registry, self.llm, self.gateway = cfg, registry, llm, gateway\n    48\t        self.index, self.approvals, self.quotas, self.audit, self.stop = index, approvals, quotas, audit, stop\n    49\t\n    50\t    def new_state(self, user_text: str, subject_token: str) -> RunState:\n    51\t        budget = RunBudget(self.cfg[\"budgets\"][\"run\"], self.cfg[\"pricing\"])\n    52\t        return RunState(messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_text}], budget=budget, subject_token=subject_token)\n    53\t\n    54\t    async def run(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState) -> RunState:\n    55\t        with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"agent.run\", attributes={\"agent.instance.id\": ctx.agent_instance_id}):\n    56\t            try:\n    57\t                await self._loop(ctx, state)\n    58\t            except BudgetExceeded as exc:\n    59\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"budget_stop\", decision=\"stop\", reason=exc.limit, parent_step_id=state.last_step)\n    60\t                state.status, state.reply = \"stopped\", \"I've reached the limit for this conversation. An HR advisor can help further.\"\n    61\t            return state\n    62\t\n    63\t    async def _loop(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState) -> None:\n    64\t        model = self.cfg[\"model\"]\n    65\t        while True:\n    66\t            await self.stop.raise_if_stopped(ctx)\n    67\t            state.budget.check_step()\n    68\t            step_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    69\t            with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"gen_ai.chat\", attributes={\"gen_ai.request.model\": model[\"id\"]}):\n    70\t                resp = await self.llm.messages.create(\n    71\t                    model=model[\"id\"], system=SYSTEM_PROMPT, max_tokens=model[\"max_output_tokens\"],\n    72\t                    temperature=model[\"temperature\"], tools=self.registry.model_schemas(), messages=state.messages,\n    73\t                    timeout=state.budget.remaining_seconds(),\n    74\t                )\n    75\t                tokens, cost = state.budget.record_usage(resp.usage)\n    76\t                ctx_tokens = resp.usage.input_tokens + (resp.usage.cache_read_input_tokens or 0) + (resp.usage.cache_creation_input_tokens or 0)\n    77\t                await self.audit.record(\n    78\t                    ctx, \"model_call\", step_id=step_id, parent_step_id=state.last_step,\n    79\t                    context_tokens=ctx_tokens, context_tokens_method=\"measured:provider_usage_input+cache_read+cache_write\",\n    80\t                    model_requested=model[\"id\"], model_served=resp.model, result={\"stop_reason\": resp.stop_reason},\n    81\t                )\n    82\t                await self.quotas.record_usage(ctx, tokens, cost)\n    83\t            state.last_step = step_id\n    84\t            state.messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [b.model_dump() for b in resp.content]})\n    85\t            if resp.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":\n    86\t                state.status = \"done\"\n    87\t                state.reply = \"\".join(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == \"text\")\n    88\t                return\n    89\t            results = []\n    90\t            for block in (b for b in resp.content if b.type == \"tool_use\"):\n    91\t                result = await self._handle_tool(ctx, state, step_id, block.id, block.name, block.input)\n    92\t                if result is None:  # paused for employee approval\n    93\t                    return\n    94\t                results.append(result)\n    95\t            state.messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": results})\n    96\t\n    97\t    async def _handle_tool(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, parent: str, tool_use_id: str,\n    98\t                           name: str, args: Any) -> dict[str, Any] | None:\n    99\t        step_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n   100\t        await self.audit.record(ctx, \"proposed_action\", step_id=step_id, parent_step_id=parent, tool=name, args=args)\n   101\t        spec = self.registry.get(name)\n   102\t        if spec is None:\n   103\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=name, decision=\"deny\", reason=\"tool not in allowlist\")\n   104\t            return _tool_result(tool_use_id, \"This tool is not available.\", error=True)\n   105\t        target = spec.target(ctx.subject)\n   106\t        state.budget.check_tool_call()\n   107\t        try:\n   108\t            args = validate_tool_args(spec, args)\n   109\t        except ValidationRejected as exc:\n   110\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   111\t                                    decision=\"deny\", reason=exc.reason, args=args)\n   112\t            return _tool_result(tool_use_id, f\"Invalid arguments: {exc.reason}\", error=True)\n   113\t        if spec.approval == \"subject\":\n   114\t            approval_id, text = await self.approvals.request(ctx, spec.qualified, target, args, tool_use_id)\n   115\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"approval_requested\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   116\t                                    decision=\"hold\", approval_id=approval_id, args=args)\n   117\t            state.status = \"awaiting_approval\"\n   118\t            state.pending_approval = {\"approval_id\": approval_id, \"description\": text}\n   119\t            return None\n   120\t        await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target, decision=\"allow\")\n   121\t        return await self._execute(ctx, state, step_id, tool_use_id, spec, target, args, approval_id=None)\n   122\t\n   123\t    async def resume_with_approval(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, approval_id: str) -> RunState:\n   124\t        with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"agent.resume\", attributes={\"agent.instance.id\": ctx.agent_instance_id}):\n   125\t            pending = await self.approvals.pending(approval_id)\n   126\t            if pending is None or pending[\"run_id\"] != ctx.run_id:\n   127\t                raise ApprovalInvalid(\"unknown approval for this run\")\n   128\t            spec = self.registry.by_qualified(pending[\"operation\"])\n   129\t            step_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n   130\t            try:\n   131\t                await self.stop.raise_if_stopped(ctx)\n   132\t                args = validate_tool_args(spec, pending[\"args\"])\n   133\t                target = spec.target(ctx.subject)\n   134\t                await self._recheck_state(ctx, state, spec, args)\n   135\t                grant = await self.approvals.consume(ctx, approval_id, spec.qualified, target, args)\n   136\t            except (ApprovalInvalid, ValidationRejected, ToolDenied, ToolError) as exc:\n   137\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=pending[\"operation\"],\n   138\t                                        decision=\"deny\", reason=str(exc), approval_id=approval_id)\n   139\t                state.status, state.reply = \"done\", \"That action can no longer be carried out as approved.\"\n   140\t                return state\n   141\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"approval_used\", step_id=step_id, parent_step_id=state.last_step,\n   142\t                                    tool=spec.qualified, target=target, decision=\"allow\", approval_id=approval_id,\n   143\t                                    result={\"approved_by\": grant[\"approved_by\"], \"approved_at\": grant[\"approved_at\"].isoformat()})\n   144\t            result = await self._execute(ctx, state, step_id, pending[\"tool_use_id\"], spec, target, args, approval_id=approval_id)\n   145\t            state.messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [result]})\n   146\t            state.status, state.pending_approval = \"running\", None\n   147\t            return await self.run(ctx, state)\n   148\t\n   149\t    async def _recheck_state(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, spec: ToolSpec, args: dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n   150\t        \"\"\"Re-read the resource the action depends on, at execution time.\"\"\"\n   151\t        if spec.qualified != \"hris/file_leave_request\" or args[\"leave_type\"] == \"unpaid\":\n   152\t            return\n   153\t        balance_spec = self.registry.get(\"get_leave_balance\")\n   154\t        bal = await self.gateway.call(balance_spec, {\"leave_type\": args[\"leave_type\"]}, ctx, state.subject_token,\n   155\t                                      operation_id=str(uuid.uuid4()))\n   156\t        bal = validate_tool_output(balance_spec, bal, self.cfg[\"limits\"][\"max_tool_output_bytes\"])\n   157\t        days = (date.fromisoformat(args[\"end_date\"]) - date.fromisoformat(args[\"start_date\"])).days + 1\n   158\t        if days <= 0 or bal[\"balance_days\"] < days:\n   159\t            raise ApprovalInvalid(\"leave balance no longer covers the request\")\n   160\t\n   161\t    async def _execute(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, step_id: str, tool_use_id: str, spec: ToolSpec,\n   162\t                       target: str, args: dict[str, Any], approval_id: str | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n   163\t        operation_id = approval_id or str(uuid.uuid4())\n   164\t        with tracer.start_as_current_span(f\"execute_tool {spec.qualified}\", attributes={\"gen_ai.tool.name\": spec.name}):\n   165\t            try:\n   166\t                if spec.kind == \"local\":\n   167\t                    raw = await self.index.search(args[\"query\"], ctx.subject)\n   168\t                    max_chars = self.cfg[\"retrieval\"][\"max_chunk_chars\"]\n   169\t                    clean: Any = []\n   170\t                    for chunk in raw:\n   171\t                        try:\n   172\t                            clean.append(validate_chunk(chunk, max_chars))\n   173\t                        except ValidationRejected as exc:\n   174\t                            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"content_held\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified,\n   175\t                                                    decision=\"hold\", reason=exc.reason, result={\"chunk_id\": chunk[\"chunk_id\"]})\n   176\t                else:\n   177\t                    raw = await self.gateway.call(spec, args, ctx, state.subject_token, operation_id=operation_id)\n   178\t                    clean = validate_tool_output(spec, raw, self.cfg[\"limits\"][\"max_tool_output_bytes\"])\n   179\t            except ToolDenied as exc:\n   180\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"tool_denied\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   181\t                                        decision=\"deny\", reason=exc.reason, approval_id=approval_id, args=args)\n   182\t                return _tool_result(tool_use_id, \"The request was refused by policy.\", error=True)\n   183\t            except ToolError as exc:\n   184\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"tool_error\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   185\t                                        decision=\"error\", reason=f\"status {exc.status}\", approval_id=approval_id,\n   186\t                                        args=args, result={\"detail\": exc.detail})\n   187\t                log.warning(\"tool error\", extra={\"tool\": spec.qualified, \"detail\": redact(exc.detail)})\n   188\t                return _tool_result(tool_use_id, f\"{spec.name} failed: {exc.detail}\", error=True)\n   189\t            except ValidationRejected as exc:\n   190\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"content_held\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   191\t                                        decision=\"hold\", reason=exc.reason, approval_id=approval_id)\n   192\t                return _tool_result(tool_use_id, \"The tool returned data that could not be used.\", error=True)\n   193\t        await self.audit.record(ctx, \"tool_result\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   194\t                                decision=\"ok\", approval_id=approval_id, args=args, result=clean)\n   195\t        envelope = {\"source\": spec.qualified, \"trust\": \"untrusted_data\", \"data\": clean}\n   196\t        return _tool_result(tool_use_id, json.dumps(envelope, ensure_ascii=False))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\tdef _tool_result(tool_use_id: str, content: str, error: bool = False) -> dict[str, Any]:\n   200\t    return {\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": tool_use_id, \"content\": content, \"is_error\": error}\n     1\t\"\"\"Hard limits enforced by the harness, outside the model.\"\"\"\n     2\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     3\t\n     4\timport time\n     5\tfrom collections import deque\n     6\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n     7\tfrom typing import Any\n     8\t\n     9\timport asyncpg\n    10\t\n    11\tfrom .audit import RunContext\n    12\t\n    13\t\n    14\tclass BudgetExceeded(Exception):\n    15\t    def __init__(self, limit: str):\n    16\t        super().__init__(limit)\n    17\t        self.limit = limit\n    18\t\n    19\t\n    20\t@dataclass\n    21\tclass RunBudget:\n    22\t    cfg: dict[str, Any]\n    23\t    pricing: dict[str, float]\n    24\t    started: float = field(default_factory=time.monotonic)\n    25\t    steps: int = 0\n    26\t    tokens: int = 0\n    27\t    cost_usd: float = 0.0\n    28\t    tool_calls: deque = field(default_factory=deque)\n    29\t\n    30\t    def check_step(self) -> None:\n    31\t        if self.steps >= self.cfg[\"max_steps\"]:\n    32\t            raise BudgetExceeded(\"max_steps\")\n    33\t        if time.monotonic() - self.started > self.cfg[\"max_wall_seconds\"]:\n    34\t            raise BudgetExceeded(\"max_wall_seconds\")\n    35\t        if self.tokens >= self.cfg[\"max_total_tokens\"]:\n    36\t            raise BudgetExceeded(\"max_total_tokens\")\n    37\t        if self.cost_usd >= self.cfg[\"max_cost_usd\"]:\n    38\t            raise BudgetExceeded(\"max_cost_usd\")\n    39\t        self.steps += 1\n    40\t\n    41\t    def remaining_seconds(self) -> float:\n    42\t        return max(0.0, self.cfg[\"max_wall_seconds\"] - (time.monotonic() - self.started))\n    43\t\n    44\t    def record_usage(self, usage: Any) -> tuple[int, float]:\n    45\t        cache_read = getattr(usage, \"cache_read_input_tokens\", 0) or 0\n    46\t        cache_write = getattr(usage, \"cache_creation_input_tokens\", 0) or 0\n    47\t        tokens_in = usage.input_tokens + cache_read + cache_write\n    48\t        tokens = tokens_in + usage.output_tokens\n    49\t        cost = (\n    50\t            (usage.input_tokens + cache_write) * self.pricing[\"input_per_mtok\"]\n    51\t            + cache_read * self.pricing[\"cache_read_per_mtok\"]\n    52\t            + usage.output_tokens * self.pricing[\"output_per_mtok\"]\n    53\t        ) / 1_000_000\n    54\t        self.tokens += tokens\n    55\t        self.cost_usd += cost\n    56\t        return tokens, cost\n    57\t\n    58\t    def check_tool_call(self) -> None:\n    59\t        now = time.monotonic()\n    60\t        while self.tool_calls and now - self.tool_calls[0] > 60:\n    61\t            self.tool_calls.popleft()\n    62\t        if len(self.tool_calls) >= self.cfg[\"max_tool_calls_per_minute\"]:\n    63\t            raise BudgetExceeded(\"max_tool_calls_per_minute\")\n    64\t        self.tool_calls.append(now)\n    65\t\n    66\t    def check_subagent(self, started_so_far: int) -> None:\n    67\t        if started_so_far >= self.cfg[\"max_subagents\"]:\n    68\t            raise BudgetExceeded(\"max_subagents\")\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t_WINDOW = \"to_timestamp(floor(extract(epoch FROM now()) / $3) * $3)\"\n    72\t\n    73\t_RESERVE_RUN = f\"\"\"\n    74\tINSERT INTO quota.usage (scope, key, window_start, runs, tokens, cost_usd)\n    75\tVALUES ($1, $2, {_WINDOW}, 1, 0, 0)\n    76\tON CONFLICT (scope, key, window_start) DO UPDATE\n    77\t   SET runs = quota.usage.runs + 1\n    78\t WHERE quota.usage.runs + 1 <= $4\n    79\t   AND quota.usage.tokens < $5\n    80\t   AND quota.usage.cost_usd < $6\n    81\tRETURNING runs\n    82\t\"\"\"\n    83\t\n    84\t_RECORD_USAGE = f\"\"\"\n    85\tINSERT INTO quota.usage (scope, key, window_start, runs, tokens, cost_usd)\n    86\tVALUES ($1, $2, {_WINDOW}, 0, $4, $5)\n    87\tON CONFLICT (scope, key, window_start) DO UPDATE\n    88\t   SET tokens = quota.usage.tokens + EXCLUDED.tokens,\n    89\t       cost_usd = quota.usage.cost_usd + EXCLUDED.cost_usd\n    90\tRETURNING tokens, cost_usd\n    91\t\"\"\"\n    92\t\n    93\t\n    94\tclass QuotaStore:\n    95\t    \"\"\"Per-user, per-tenant and fleet-wide quotas, shared by every replica.\"\"\"\n    96\t\n    97\t    def __init__(self, pool: asyncpg.Pool, quotas: list[dict[str, Any]]):\n    98\t        self._pool = pool\n    99\t        self._quotas = quotas\n   100\t\n   101\t    def _key(self, scope: str, ctx: RunContext) -> str:\n   102\t        return {\"user\": ctx.subject, \"tenant\": ctx.tenant, \"fleet\": \"all\"}[scope]\n   103\t\n   104\t    async def reserve_run(self, ctx: RunContext) -> None:\n   105\t        async with self._pool.acquire() as conn, conn.transaction():\n   106\t            for q in self._quotas:\n   107\t                row = await conn.fetchrow(\n   108\t                    _RESERVE_RUN, q[\"scope\"], self._key(q[\"scope\"], ctx), q[\"window_seconds\"],\n   109\t                    q[\"max_runs\"], q[\"max_tokens\"], q[\"max_cost_usd\"],\n   110\t                )\n   111\t                if row is None:\n   112\t                    raise BudgetExceeded(f\"quota:{q['scope']}\")\n   113\t\n   114\t    async def record_usage(self, ctx: RunContext, tokens: int, cost_usd: float) -> None:\n   115\t        \"\"\"Usage already spent is always recorded; the run stops once any window is over its limit.\"\"\"\n   116\t        exceeded: str | None = None\n   117\t        async with self._pool.acquire() as conn, conn.transaction():\n   118\t            for q in self._quotas:\n   119\t                row = await conn.fetchrow(\n   120\t                    _RECORD_USAGE, q[\"scope\"], self._key(q[\"scope\"], ctx), q[\"window_seconds\"], tokens, cost_usd\n   121\t                )\n   122\t                if row[\"tokens\"] >= q[\"max_tokens\"] or row[\"cost_usd\"] >= q[\"max_cost_usd\"]:\n   123\t                    exceeded = exceeded or f\"quota:{q['scope']}\"\n   124\t        if exceeded:\n   125\t            raise BudgetExceeded(exceeded)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d82a645-8c23-4f20-8807-403197c900cd","uuid":"8b1aa61a-eda9-4d3c-b58b-a14bc80ed73d","timestamp":"2026-11-21T11:26:15.901Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t\"\"\"Approval records for consequential tool calls.\n     2\t\n     3\tAn approval is bound to one agent instance, one operation, one target and the SHA-256 of\n     4\tthe canonical arguments. Only the employee on whose behalf the run acts may approve, they\n     5\tsee a rendered description of the exact action, and each approval is consumed once.\n     6\t\"\"\"\n     7\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     8\t\n     9\timport hashlib\n    10\timport json\n    11\timport uuid\n    12\tfrom datetime import timedelta\n    13\tfrom typing import Any\n    14\t\n    15\timport asyncpg\n    16\t\n    17\tfrom .audit import RunContext\n    18\t\n    19\t\n    20\tclass ApprovalInvalid(Exception):\n    21\t    pass\n    22\t\n    23\t\n    24\tdef args_digest(args: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    25\t    canonical = json.dumps(args, sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"), ensure_ascii=False)\n    26\t    return hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode()).hexdigest()\n    27\t\n    28\t\n    29\tdef describe(operation: str, target: str, args: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    30\t    if operation == \"hris/file_leave_request\":\n    31\t        return (f\"Submit a {args['leave_type']} leave request from {args['start_date']} to {args['end_date']} \"\n    32\t                f\"for {target}. Your manager will be asked to approve it in HRIS.\")\n    33\t    if operation == \"helpdesk/create_ticket\":\n    34\t        return f\"Send this summary to an HR advisor ({args['category']}):\\n\\n{args['summary']}\"\n    35\t    raise ValueError(operation)\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tclass ApprovalStore:\n    39\t    def __init__(self, pool: asyncpg.Pool, ttl_seconds: int):\n    40\t        self._pool = pool\n    41\t        self._ttl = timedelta(seconds=ttl_seconds)\n    42\t\n    43\t    async def request(self, ctx: RunContext, operation: str, target: str, args: dict[str, Any], tool_use_id: str) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    44\t        approval_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    45\t        text = describe(operation, target, args)\n    46\t        await self._pool.execute(\n    47\t            \"\"\"INSERT INTO approvals.grants\n    48\t                 (approval_id, run_id, agent_instance_id, subject, operation, target, args_sha256, args,\n    49\t                  tool_use_id, description, requested_at, expires_at)\n    50\t               VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5, $6, $7, $8::jsonb, $9, $10, now(), now() + $11)\"\"\",\n    51\t            approval_id, ctx.run_id, ctx.agent_instance_id, ctx.subject, operation, target,\n    52\t            args_digest(args), json.dumps(args), tool_use_id, text, self._ttl,\n    53\t        )\n    54\t        return approval_id, text\n    55\t\n    56\t    async def confirm(self, approval_id: str, approver: str) -> None:\n    57\t        \"\"\"Called from the employee's authenticated session. Approver must be the run's subject.\"\"\"\n    58\t        row = await self._pool.fetchrow(\n    59\t            \"\"\"UPDATE approvals.grants SET approved_by = $2, approved_at = now()\n    60\t                WHERE approval_id = $1 AND subject = $2 AND approved_at IS NULL AND expires_at > now()\n    61\t                RETURNING approval_id\"\"\",\n    62\t            approval_id, approver,\n    63\t        )\n    64\t        if row is None:\n    65\t            raise ApprovalInvalid(\"not found, expired, already decided, or approver lacks authority\")\n    66\t\n    67\t    async def consume(self, ctx: RunContext, approval_id: str, operation: str, target: str, args: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    68\t        \"\"\"Atomically mark the approval used. Every binding is re-checked in the same statement.\"\"\"\n    69\t        row = await self._pool.fetchrow(\n    70\t            \"\"\"UPDATE approvals.grants SET used_at = now()\n    71\t                WHERE approval_id = $1\n    72\t                  AND used_at IS NULL\n    73\t                  AND approved_at IS NOT NULL\n    74\t                  AND approved_by = subject\n    75\t                  AND expires_at > now()\n    76\t                  AND agent_instance_id = $2\n    77\t                  AND subject = $3\n    78\t                  AND operation = $4\n    79\t                  AND target = $5\n    80\t                  AND args_sha256 = $6\n    81\t                RETURNING approval_id, approved_by, approved_at, expires_at\"\"\",\n    82\t            approval_id, ctx.agent_instance_id, ctx.subject, operation, target, args_digest(args),\n    83\t        )\n    84\t        if row is None:\n    85\t            raise ApprovalInvalid(\"approval does not match this action, was used, or expired\")\n    86\t        return dict(row)\n    87\t\n    88\t    async def pending(self, approval_id: str) -> dict[str, Any] | None:\n    89\t        row = await self._pool.fetchrow(\n    90\t            \"SELECT run_id, operation, target, args, tool_use_id FROM approvals.grants WHERE approval_id = $1\",\n    91\t            approval_id,\n    92\t        )\n    93\t        if row is None:\n    94\t            return None\n    95\t        out = dict(row)\n    96\t        out[\"args\"] = json.loads(out[\"args\"])\n    97\t        return out\n     1\t\"\"\"Agent loop for the HR helpdesk.\"\"\"\n     2\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     3\t\n     4\timport json\n     5\timport logging\n     6\timport pathlib\n     7\timport uuid\n     8\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n     9\tfrom datetime import date\n    10\tfrom typing import Any\n    11\t\n    12\tfrom anthropic import AsyncAnthropic\n    13\tfrom opentelemetry import trace\n    14\t\n    15\tfrom .approvals import ApprovalInvalid, ApprovalStore\n    16\tfrom .audit import AuditWriter, RunContext\n    17\tfrom .budgets import BudgetExceeded, QuotaStore, RunBudget\n    18\tfrom .gateway_client import GatewayClient, ToolDenied, ToolError\n    19\tfrom .killswitch import StopControl\n    20\tfrom .redact import redact\n    21\tfrom .retrieval import PolicyIndex\n    22\tfrom .tools import ToolRegistry, ToolSpec\n    23\tfrom .validation import ValidationRejected, validate_chunk, validate_tool_args, validate_tool_output\n    24\t\n    25\tlog = logging.getLogger(\"hr_agent.loop\")\n    26\ttracer = trace.get_tracer(\"hr_agent\")\n    27\t\n    28\tSYSTEM_PROMPT = (pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"prompts\" / \"system.md\").read_text()\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t@dataclass\n    32\tclass RunState:\n    33\t    messages: list[dict[str, Any]]\n    34\t    budget: RunBudget\n    35\t    subject_token: str\n    36\t    status: str = \"running\"\n    37\t    reply: str | None = None\n    38\t    pending_approval: dict[str, Any] | None = None\n    39\t    last_step: str | None = None\n    40\t    subagents_started: int = 0\n    41\t    extra: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tclass Agent:\n    45\t    def __init__(self, cfg: dict[str, Any], registry: ToolRegistry, llm: AsyncAnthropic, gateway: GatewayClient,\n    46\t                 index: PolicyIndex, approvals: ApprovalStore, quotas: QuotaStore, audit: AuditWriter, stop: StopControl):\n    47\t        self.cfg, self.registry, self.llm, self.gateway = cfg, registry, llm, gateway\n    48\t        self.index, self.approvals, self.quotas, self.audit, self.stop = index, approvals, quotas, audit, stop\n    49\t\n    50\t    def new_state(self, user_text: str, subject_token: str) -> RunState:\n    51\t        budget = RunBudget(self.cfg[\"budgets\"][\"run\"], self.cfg[\"pricing\"])\n    52\t        return RunState(messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_text}], budget=budget, subject_token=subject_token)\n    53\t\n    54\t    async def run(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState) -> RunState:\n    55\t        with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"agent.run\", attributes={\"agent.instance.id\": ctx.agent_instance_id}):\n    56\t            try:\n    57\t                await self._loop(ctx, state)\n    58\t            except BudgetExceeded as exc:\n    59\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"budget_stop\", decision=\"stop\", reason=exc.limit, parent_step_id=state.last_step)\n    60\t                state.status, state.reply = \"stopped\", \"I've reached the limit for this conversation. An HR advisor can help further.\"\n    61\t            return state\n    62\t\n    63\t    async def _loop(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState) -> None:\n    64\t        model = self.cfg[\"model\"]\n    65\t        while True:\n    66\t            await self.stop.raise_if_stopped(ctx)\n    67\t            state.budget.check_step()\n    68\t            step_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    69\t            with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"gen_ai.chat\", attributes={\"gen_ai.request.model\": model[\"id\"]}):\n    70\t                resp = await self.llm.messages.create(\n    71\t                    model=model[\"id\"], system=SYSTEM_PROMPT, max_tokens=model[\"max_output_tokens\"],\n    72\t                    temperature=model[\"temperature\"], tools=self.registry.model_schemas(), messages=state.messages,\n    73\t                    timeout=state.budget.remaining_seconds(),\n    74\t                )\n    75\t                tokens, cost = state.budget.record_usage(resp.usage)\n    76\t                ctx_tokens = resp.usage.input_tokens + (resp.usage.cache_read_input_tokens or 0) + (resp.usage.cache_creation_input_tokens or 0)\n    77\t                await self.audit.record(\n    78\t                    ctx, \"model_call\", step_id=step_id, parent_step_id=state.last_step,\n    79\t                    context_tokens=ctx_tokens, context_tokens_method=\"measured:provider_usage_input+cache_read+cache_write\",\n    80\t                    model_requested=model[\"id\"], model_served=resp.model, result={\"stop_reason\": resp.stop_reason},\n    81\t                )\n    82\t                await self.quotas.record_usage(ctx, tokens, cost)\n    83\t            state.last_step = step_id\n    84\t            state.messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [b.model_dump() for b in resp.content]})\n    85\t            if resp.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":\n    86\t                state.status = \"done\"\n    87\t                state.reply = \"\".join(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == \"text\")\n    88\t                return\n    89\t            results = []\n    90\t            for block in (b for b in resp.content if b.type == \"tool_use\"):\n    91\t                result = await self._handle_tool(ctx, state, step_id, block.id, block.name, block.input)\n    92\t                if result is None:  # paused for employee approval\n    93\t                    return\n    94\t                results.append(result)\n    95\t            state.messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": results})\n    96\t\n    97\t    async def _handle_tool(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, parent: str, tool_use_id: str,\n    98\t                           name: str, args: Any) -> dict[str, Any] | None:\n    99\t        step_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n   100\t        await self.audit.record(ctx, \"proposed_action\", step_id=step_id, parent_step_id=parent, tool=name, args=args)\n   101\t        spec = self.registry.get(name)\n   102\t        if spec is None:\n   103\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=name, decision=\"deny\", reason=\"tool not in allowlist\")\n   104\t            return _tool_result(tool_use_id, \"This tool is not available.\", error=True)\n   105\t        target = spec.target(ctx.subject)\n   106\t        state.budget.check_tool_call()\n   107\t        try:\n   108\t            args = validate_tool_args(spec, args)\n   109\t        except ValidationRejected as exc:\n   110\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   111\t                                    decision=\"deny\", reason=exc.reason, args=args)\n   112\t            return _tool_result(tool_use_id, f\"Invalid arguments: {exc.reason}\", error=True)\n   113\t        if spec.approval == \"subject\":\n   114\t            approval_id, text = await self.approvals.request(ctx, spec.qualified, target, args, tool_use_id)\n   115\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"approval_requested\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target,\n   116\t                                    decision=\"hold\", approval_id=approval_id, args=args)\n   117\t            state.status = \"awaiting_approval\"\n   118\t            state.pending_approval = {\"approval_id\": approval_id, \"description\": text}\n   119\t            return None\n   120\t        await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified, target=target, decision=\"allow\")\n   121\t        return await self._execute(ctx, state, step_id, tool_use_id, spec, target, args, approval_id=None)\n   122\t\n   123\t    async def resume_with_approval(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, approval_id: str) -> RunState:\n   124\t        with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"agent.resume\", attributes={\"agent.instance.id\": ctx.agent_instance_id}):\n   125\t            pending = await self.approvals.pending(approval_id)\n   126\t            if pending is None or pending[\"run_id\"] != ctx.run_id:\n   127\t                raise ApprovalInvalid(\"unknown approval for this run\")\n   128\t            spec = self.registry.by_qualified(pending[\"operation\"])\n   129\t            step_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n   130\t            try:\n   131\t                await self.stop.raise_if_stopped(ctx)\n   132\t                args = validate_tool_args(spec, pending[\"args\"])\n   133\t                target = spec.target(ctx.subject)\n   134\t                await self._recheck_state(ctx, state, spec, args)\n   135\t                grant = await self.approvals.consume(ctx, approval_id, spec.qualified, target, args)\n   136\t            except (ApprovalInvalid, ValidationRejected, ToolDenied, ToolError) as exc:\n   137\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"authz_decision\", step_id=step_id, tool=pending[\"operation\"],\n   138\t                                        decision=\"deny\", reason=str(exc), approval_id=approval_id)\n   139\t                state.status, state.reply = \"done\", \"That action can no longer be carried out as approved.\"\n   140\t                return state\n   141\t            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"approval_used\", step_id=step_id, parent_step_id=state.last_step,\n   142\t                                    tool=spec.qualified, target=target, decision=\"allow\", approval_id=approval_id,\n   143\t                                    result={\"approved_by\": grant[\"approved_by\"], \"approved_at\": grant[\"approved_at\"].isoformat()})\n   144\t            result = await self._execute(ctx, state, step_id, pending[\"tool_use_id\"], spec, target, args, approval_id=approval_id)\n   145\t            state.messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [result]})\n   146\t            state.status, state.pending_approval = \"running\", None\n   147\t            return await self.run(ctx, state)\n   148\t\n   149\t    async def _recheck_state(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, spec: ToolSpec, args: dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n   150\t        \"\"\"Re-read the resource the action depends on, at execution time.\"\"\"\n   151\t        if spec.qualified != \"hris/file_leave_request\" or args[\"leave_type\"] == \"unpaid\":\n   152\t            return\n   153\t        balance_spec = self.registry.get(\"get_leave_balance\")\n   154\t        bal = await self.gateway.call(balance_spec, {\"leave_type\": args[\"leave_type\"]}, ctx, state.subject_token,\n   155\t                                      operation_id=str(uuid.uuid4()))\n   156\t        bal = validate_tool_output(balance_spec, bal, self.cfg[\"limits\"][\"max_tool_output_bytes\"])\n   157\t        days = (date.fromisoformat(args[\"end_date\"]) - date.fromisoformat(args[\"start_date\"])).days + 1\n   158\t        if days <= 0 or bal[\"balance_days\"] < days:\n   159\t            raise ApprovalInvalid(\"leave balance no longer covers the request\")\n   160\t\n   161\t    async def _execute(self, ctx: RunContext, state: RunState, step_id: str, tool_use_id: str, spec: ToolSpec,\n   162\t                       target: str, args: dict[str, Any], approval_id: str | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n   163\t        operation_id = approval_id or str(uuid.uuid4())\n   164\t        with tracer.start_as_current_span(f\"execute_tool {spec.qualified}\", attributes={\"gen_ai.tool.name\": spec.name}):\n   165\t            try:\n   166\t                if spec.kind == \"local\":\n   167\t                    raw = await self.index.search(args[\"query\"], ctx.subject)\n   168\t                    max_chars = self.cfg[\"retrieval\"][\"max_chunk_chars\"]\n   169\t                    clean: Any = []\n   170\t                    for chunk in raw:\n   171\t                        try:\n   172\t                            clean.append(validate_chunk(chunk, max_chars))\n   173\t                        except ValidationRejected as exc:\n   174\t                            await self.audit.record(ctx, \"content_held\", step_id=step_id, tool=spec.qualified,\n   175\t                                                    decision=\"hold\", reason=exc.reason, result={\"chunk_id\": chunk[\"chunk_id\"]})\n   176\t                else:\n   177\t                    raw = await self.gateway.call(spec, args, ctx, state.subject_token, operation_id=operation_id)\n   178\t                    clean = validate_tool_output(spec, raw, self.cfg[\"limits\"][\"max_tool_output_bytes\"])\n   179\t            except ToolDenied as exc:\n   180\t                await self.audit.record(ctx, \"tool_denied\", step_id=step